&@q•Fá]Hô|‹×aé˜LBE¯ƒ¥Jk—·ï·ÍS{"rendered":{"html":"<p>A program fails when it hits a runtime error: a <code>panic</code>, an index out of\nrange, a program it runs exiting with an error, or a failed <code>assert</code>.\nNormally that ends the run. Two constructs work with failure directly:\n<code>assert</code>, which fails on purpose when something doesn't hold, and\n<code>recover</code>, which catches a failure and continues.</p>\n<h2 id=\"failure--asserting\">Asserting <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#failure--asserting\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>assert COND</code> fails the run if the condition is false. The error shows\nthe condition as you wrote it, and for a comparison it labels each side\nwith its value, so a failed check explains itself:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"text\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>error: assertion failed: `output == \"hello\"`\n --&gt; app.gcsl:2:8\n  |\n2 | assert output == \"hello\"\n  |        ------    ------- this is `\"hello\"`\n  |        |\n  |        this is `\"hullo\"`\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Give your own message after a comma, <code>assert ok, \"the server did not start\"</code>. Each side of a comparison is evaluated only once, so an\nassertion about a program's output doesn't run the program twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"failure--recovering\">Recovering <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#failure--recovering\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>recover { BODY } else { FALLBACK }</code> evaluates the body, and if it fails,\nevaluates the fallback instead. Name the failure to read its message:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn half(n: Int): Int {\n    assert n % 2 == 0, \"{n} is odd\"\n    n // 2\n}\n\neven = recover { half(10) } else { 0 }\nodd = recover { half(7) } else { -1 }\nmessage = recover { \"half is {half(7)}\" } else (failure) { failure.message }\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The failure is a record, <code>{message: String}</code>. The two blocks join as an\n<code>if</code>'s branches do, so they normally have one type.</p>\n<p><code>recover</code> catches what <code>#[retry]</code> would retry, and nothing else. A bug in\nGCSL itself is never caught. Neither is a timeout of a call that encloses\nthe <code>recover</code>: that timeout belongs to the enclosing call, which ends as\nif the <code>recover</code> weren't there. A <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> or <code>return</code> inside\nthe body is not a failure, and leaves through it as usual. Anything the\nbody did before it failed stays done.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"failure--asserting","title":"Asserting","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"failure--recovering","title":"Recovering","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[],"anchors":[]},"refs":null}